<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

	<!--
		The papillon's cocoon (CASTES-PLAN.md §7). A greenworm who has spent enough mana spins it,
		sits inside it for fifteen days, and comes out a papillon - see
		Source/Insects/PapillonMaturation.cs for the whole chain and the four decisions behind it.

		Why a building and not a hediff: a hediff cannot render anything on its own. This is spawned
		on her own tile, and the `PassThroughOnly` passability is what lets the two share a cell -
		the same trick the abaddon's egg sac uses, and the same art, which is deliberate: the sac is
		what a player already associates with a brood turning into something else.

		The sac's own rule is that only a `KillFinalize` counts (see Things_EggSpew.xml). This one is
		the opposite, on purpose: the comp kills the woman inside it when the cocoon is broken at
		all, except for the `Vanish` mode it uses to remove itself on hatching. That was the user's
		ruling, 2026-10-02: she dies with her cocoon.
	-->
	<ThingDef ParentName="BuildingNaturalBase">
		<defName>PMM_Cocoon</defName>
		<label>papillon cocoon</label>
		<description>A papery cocoon spun by a greenworm, with something moving faintly inside it. Break it before its time and whatever is in there dies with it.</description>
		<thingClass>Building</thingClass>
		<category>Building</category>
		<graphicData>
			<texPath>Things/Building/PMM_EggSac</texPath>
			<graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
		</graphicData>
		<altitudeLayer>Building</altitudeLayer>
		<passability>PassThroughOnly</passability>
		<fillPercent>0.3</fillPercent>
		<rotatable>false</rotatable>
		<terrainAffordanceNeeded>Light</terrainAffordanceNeeded>
		<statBases>
			<!-- Softer than the egg sac: a cocoon is paper and silk, and fifteen days is a long time
			     to ask a raid to leave something alone. -->
			<MaxHitPoints>80</MaxHitPoints>
			<WorkToBuild>0</WorkToBuild>
		</statBases>
		<comps>
			<li Class="PMM_Insects.CompProperties_PapillonCocoon">
				<hediffDef>PMM_Cocooned</hediffDef>
			</li>
		</comps>
	</ThingDef>

	<!--
		The immobility that goes with it. It lives and dies with the cocoon: the cocoon's comp puts
		it on her and takes it off again, so a grub can never be left holding a cocoon that is gone
		or walking around inside one that is not.

		Moving is set to 0 rather than reduced, because "held still" is the whole point - and it is
		visible on purpose: a player who sees the hediff knows why she is not moving, and the cocoon
		beside her confirms it.

		It is also the flag the rescue gate reads (`CocoonedNotRescuedPatch`). A pawn with `Moving` at
		0 is *downed*, and vanilla carries downed colonists to a bed - the first build had her fetched
		out of her own cocoon within minutes of it closing (user's report, 2026-10-02).

		Hunger and rest stop while it is on her, which is not a comfort setting. Vanilla only feeds a
		patient who is in bed, so a woman downed in a field would have spent fifteen days starving to
		death inside her own cocoon: she goes in fed (see `BeginCocoon` in
		Source/Insects/PapillonMaturation.cs) and neither eats nor sleeps until it opens. No sleep
		also means no sleeping bubble floating over the sac she is meant to be sealed inside.
	-->
	<HediffDef>
		<defName>PMM_Cocooned</defName>
		<label>cocooned</label>
		<description>She has spun a cocoon and is changing inside it. She cannot move until it opens.</description>
		<hediffClass>HediffWithComps</hediffClass>
		<isBad>false</isBad>
		<scenarioCanAdd>false</scenarioCanAdd>
		<stages>
			<li>
				<capMods>
					<li>
						<capacity>Moving</capacity>
						<setMax>0</setMax>
					</li>
				</capMods>
				<hungerRateFactor>0</hungerRateFactor>
				<restFallFactor>0</restFallFactor>
			</li>
		</stages>
	</HediffDef>

</Defs>
